<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valentinian III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">419–<a href="/years/455">455</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Flavius Placidus Valentinianus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valentinian III was Western Roman emperor from 425 to 455, placed on the throne as a child with the support of the Eastern court and ruled at first under the regency of his mother Galla Placidia. His reign saw the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, while real power lay with the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>. Valentinian murdered Aetius in 454 and was himself killed by Aetius's followers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 455.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii.jpg" alt="Valentinian III" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tony Querrec (photo) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Valentinian_III,_Louvre_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Valentinian was born at Ravenna on 2 July 419, the only son of Galla Placidia and Constantius III. Through his mother he descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a>, and he was nephew of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> and cousin of the eastern emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a>. After Constantius died in 421, court intrigue forced Galla Placidia to flee with her children to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. When Honorius died in 423 and Joannes took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Theodosius named the five-year-old Valentinian caesar for the West in October 424 and betrothed him to his daughter Licinia Eudoxia. After Joannes was defeated, the eastern official Helion installed Valentinian as augustus in Rome on 23 October 425, at the age of six.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under his mother's influence the early reign saw victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> left Pannonia Valeria. A struggle among the generals Felix, Bonifatius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> troubled it: Aetius had Felix killed in 430, and Bonifatius, who had brought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, was beaten and fled. Valentinian made peace with the Vandal king Gaiseric in 435. He married Licinia Eudoxia in 437 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and the regency ended, but policy remained in the hands of Aetius. The Vandals took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> on 19 October 439, a major blow because taxes and food from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 444 the government taxed sales and the senatorial class to pay for troops.</p><p>In 451 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> gathered a coalition and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains">Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</a>. In 452 Attila invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>. Valentinian sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-i">Pope Leo I</a> and two senators to negotiate, and plague, famine and an attack by the eastern emperor Marcian on Hun homelands made Attila withdraw.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Egged on by the senator Petronius Maximus and his chamberlain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, Valentinian killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> with his own hands on 21 September 454. A counsellor reportedly told him that he had cut off his right hand with his left. On 16 March 455 two of Aetius's followers, Optelas and Thraustelas, killed him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a>. Petronius Maximus was proclaimed emperor and died after eleven weeks, and Gaiseric's Vandals then sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for two weeks. By Valentinian's death almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> had left Roman control. He is described as spoiled and pleasure-loving, yet devoted to religion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> and J. B. Bury judged him unfavourably.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valentinian III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He claimed Honoria, sister of <b>Valentinian III</b>, as his bride with half the Western Roman Empire as dowry, and in 451 entered Gaul, where Romans and Visigoths fought him at the Catalaunian Plains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Peace came in 435 through a treaty between <b>Valentinian III</b> and Gaiseric, which gave the Vandals coastal Numidia and parts of Mauretania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A fire in 217 destroyed the wooden upper levels, and repairs followed until about 240, in 250 or 252 and in 320; an inscription records restoration under Theodosius II and <b>Valentinian III</b>, possibly after an earthquake in 443.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>With the support of the Senate, Maximus secured the throne after the murder of <b>Valentinian III</b> by paying palace officials, and married the reluctant widow Licinia Eudoxia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-17">17 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Valentinian III</b> killed Flavius Aetius with his own hands during a meeting in Ravenna.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ravenna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=442" aria-label="Ravenna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Valentinian III"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5740 3741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5740 3741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=442">What was here in 442 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
